00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:45.110
Let's start with the first [NOISE].

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:50.180
So one way we can decompose the kinds of things AI can do in healthcare is

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:56.505
by trying to formulate levels of questions that we can ask from data.

00:01:56.505 --> 00:01:58.970
At the lowest level are

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:00.710
what are descriptive questions.

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:03.870
Here, we're really trying to get at what happened.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:08.885
Then there are diagnostic questions where we're asking why did it happen?

00:02:08.885 --> 00:02:11.950
If a patient had chest pains, I took their,

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.690
[NOISE] x-ray, what does that chest x-ray show?

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:17.825
If they have palpitations,

00:02:17.825 --> 00:02:19.960
what does their ECG show?

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.440
Then there are predictive problems.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.640
Here, I care about asking about the future,

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.680
what's going to happen in the next six months?

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.920
[NOISE] And then at the highest level, are prescriptive problems.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.425
Here, I'm really trying to ask, okay,

00:02:34.425 --> 00:02:36.345
I know this is the patient,

00:02:36.345 --> 00:02:39.530
this is the symptoms they're coming in with, this is how, ah,

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.640
their trajectory will look like in terms of,

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:48.325
um, in terms of things that may happen that they're at risk of, what should I do?

00:02:48.325 --> 00:02:50.465
And this is the real action point.

00:02:50.465 --> 00:02:53.285
And that's I would say the,

00:02:53.285 --> 00:02:54.620
the gold mine but,

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:58.790
ah, to get there requires a lot of data and a lot of steps.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:01.620
And we'll talk a little bit more about that.

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:07.830
So in CS230 you're all well aware,

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:12.695
ah, of the paradigm shift of, of deep learning.

00:03:12.695 --> 00:03:15.610
And if we look at machine-learning in,

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:17.590
um, healthcare literature,

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:21.860
we see that it has a very similar pattern is that we

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:27.309
had this feature extraction engineer who was responsible for,

00:03:27.309 --> 00:03:33.050
um, getting from the input to a set of features that a classifier can understand,

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:35.690
and the deep learning paradigm is to combine

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:39.595
feature extraction and the classification into one step,

00:03:39.595 --> 00:03:43.040
ah, by automatically extracting features, which is cool.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.815
Here's what I think will be the next paradigm, ah,

00:03:45.815 --> 00:03:48.890
shift for AI in healthcare,

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:51.025
but also more generally.

00:03:51.025 --> 00:03:54.770
Is, ah, we still have a deep learning engineer up here,

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:57.480
ah, that's you, that's me, ah,

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:58.730
that are designing the networks,

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:00.320
that are making decisions like

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.780
a convolutional neural network is the best architecture for this problem,

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:05.885
this specific type of architecture.

00:04:05.885 --> 00:04:10.220
There's an RNN and CNN and whatever NN you can throw on there.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:16.440
But, what if we could just replace out the ML engineer as well?

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.690
Ah, and I find this quite funny,

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:20.240
because everyone, you know,

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:22.940
in AI for healthcare, a question that I get asked a lot is,

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.300
are we going to replace doctors with all these AI solutions?

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:30.275
And nobody actually realizes that we might replace

00:04:30.275 --> 00:04:34.290
machine-learning engineers faster than we might replace doctors if this,

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:35.735
er, is to be the case.

00:04:35.735 --> 00:04:37.970
And a lot of research is, ah,

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:41.670
developing algorithms that can automatically learn architectures,

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:44.495
some of which you might go through in this class.

00:04:44.495 --> 00:04:47.705
Great. So that's the general overview.

00:04:47.705 --> 00:04:50.990
Now I wanna talk about three case studies in the lab

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:54.855
[NOISE] of AI being applied to different problems.

00:04:54.855 --> 00:05:00.875
And because healthcare is so broad I thought I'd focus in on one narrow vertical,

00:05:00.875 --> 00:05:04.965
and let us go deep on that and that's medical imaging.

00:05:04.965 --> 00:05:08.285
So I've chosen three problems, um,

00:05:08.285 --> 00:05:11.945
and one of them is a 1D problem,

00:05:11.945 --> 00:05:14.645
the second is a 2D problem is, and the third is a,

00:05:14.645 --> 00:05:16.220
is a t- 3D problem, so

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:20.270
I thought we could- we can walk through [NOISE] all the different kinds of data here.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:25.880
Ah, so this is some work that was done early last year in

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:28.610
the lab where we showed that we were

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:32.390
able to detect arrhythmias at the level of cardiologists.

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:36.080
Um, so arrhythmias are an important problem,

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:37.760
affect millions of people, ah,

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:41.580
this has especially come to light recently with the, ah,

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:46.565
devices, like the Apple Watch which now have a ECG monitoring.

00:05:46.565 --> 00:05:50.390
Ah, and, ah, the thing about this is that

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:54.350
sometimes you might have symptoms and know that you have arrhythmias,

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:55.975
but other times, ah,

00:05:55.975 --> 00:06:00.740
you may not have symptoms and still have arrhythmias that can be addressed with,

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:03.770
ah, with, ah, if,

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:05.785
if you were to do an ECG.

00:06:05.785 --> 00:06:10.550
An ECG's test is basically showing the heart's electrical activity over time.

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:12.890
The electrodes are attached to the skin,

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:16.080
safe tests and it takes over a few minutes,

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:19.400
and this is what it looks like when you're hooked up to all the different electrodes.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:25.340
Ah, so this test is often done for a few minutes in the hospital,

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:28.430
[NOISE] and the finding is basically that, uh,

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:33.170
in a few minutes you can't really capture a person's abnormal heart rhythms.

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:37.520
So let's send them home for 24 to 48 hours with a Holter monitor,

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:39.500
and let's see what we can find.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:44.930
Um, there are more recent devices such as the Zio Patch, which let, um,

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:47.690
let patients be monitored for up to two weeks,

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:49.970
and [NOISE] it's, it's quite convenient.

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:52.490
You can use it in the shower or while you're sleeping,

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:54.275
so you really can capture, ah,

00:06:54.275 --> 00:06:57.830
a lot of what- what's happening in the hearts,

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:01.170
uh, uh, ECG activity.

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:05.660
But if we look at the amount of data that's generated in two weeks,

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:07.955
it's 1.6 million heartbeats.

00:07:07.955 --> 00:07:12.110
That's a lot, and there are very few doctors who'd be

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:16.820
willing to go through two weeks of ECG reading for each of their patients.

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:21.635
And this really motivates why we need automated interpretation here.

00:07:21.635 --> 00:07:25.635
But automated detection comes with its challenges.

00:07:25.635 --> 00:07:28.210
One of them is, you know,

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:31.235
you have in the hospital several electrodes,

00:07:31.235 --> 00:07:34.100
and in more recent devices we have just one.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:38.630
Ah, and the way one can think of several electrodes is sort

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:43.970
of th- the electrical activity of the heart is 3D, and, um,

00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:49.100
each one of the electrodes is giving a different 2D perspective into the 3D perspective,

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:52.300
um, but now that we'll have only one lead,

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:54.710
we only have one of these perspectives available.

00:07:54.710 --> 00:08:00.935
Ah, and the second one is that the differences between the heart rhythms are very subtle.

00:08:00.935 --> 00:08:03.305
This is what a cardiac cycle looks like,

00:08:03.305 --> 00:08:05.210
and when we're looking at, ah,

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:09.250
[NOISE] arrhythmias or abnormal heart rhythms, ah,

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:14.990
one's going to look at the substructures within the cycle and then,

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:18.335
ah, this structure between cycles as well.

00:08:18.335 --> 00:08:22.200
And the differences are, are quite subtle.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:27.910
So when we started working on this problem,

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:29.815
oh, maybe I should share this story.

00:08:29.815 --> 00:08:33.460
So, uh, we started working on this problem and then it was, uh,

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:36.085
me, my, my collaborator Awni and,

00:08:36.085 --> 00:08:37.795
uh, and Professor Ng.

00:08:37.795 --> 00:08:39.790
And one of the things that he,

00:08:39.790 --> 00:08:41.845
that he mentioned we should do he said,

00:08:41.845 --> 00:08:45.730
"Let's just go out and read ECG books and let's do the exercises."

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:46.915
And if you're in med school,

00:08:46.915 --> 00:08:48.070
there are these, uh,

00:08:48.070 --> 00:08:50.740
books where, where you can, uh,

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:53.200
where you can learn about ECG interpretation and

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:56.050
then there are several exercises that you can do to test yourselves.

00:08:56.050 --> 00:08:58.690
Uh, so I went to the med school library,

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:00.610
uh, you know, they have those,

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:02.685
uh, uh, hand-cranked, uh,

00:09:02.685 --> 00:09:06.150
shelves at the bottom so you have to move them and then grab my book.

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:09.630
And then we went for two weeks and did, uh, learned,

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:15.110
so did go through two books and learned ECG interpretation and it was pretty challenging.

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:19.660
And if we looked at previous literature to this,

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:20.920
I think they were, sort of,

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:26.530
drawing upon some domain knowledge here and that here we are looking at waves.

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:32.070
How can we extract specific features from waves that doctors are also looking at?

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:34.770
So there was a lot of feature engineering going on.

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:37.470
And if you're familiar with wavelet transforms, they were the,

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:41.530
sort of, um, they were the most common approach,

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:43.570
uh, with a lot of, sort of, like,

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:45.160
different mother wavelets etc., etc.,

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:48.595
pre-processing band pass filters.

00:09:48.595 --> 00:09:51.880
So everything you can imagine doing with signals was done.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:55.520
And then you fed it into your SVM and you called it a day.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:59.020
With deep learning, we can change things up a bit.

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:04.465
So on the left, we have an ECG signal and on the right as just, uh,

00:10:04.465 --> 00:10:06.940
three heart rhythms, we're gonna call them A, B,

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:12.530
and C. And we're gonna learn a mapping to go straight from the input to the output.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:15.865
And here's how we're gonna break it out.

00:10:15.865 --> 00:10:18.775
We're gonna say that every label,

00:10:18.775 --> 00:10:22.525
labels the same amount of the signal.

00:10:22.525 --> 00:10:27.295
So if we had four labels and the ECG would be split into these four,

00:10:27.295 --> 00:10:31.195
sort of, this rhythm is labeling this part.

00:10:31.195 --> 00:10:35.480
And then we're gonna use a deep neural network.

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:39.969
So we built a 1D convolutional neural network,

00:10:39.969 --> 00:10:44.200
uh, which runs over the time dimension of the input.

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:46.480
Because remember, we're getting one scalar,

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:48.805
uh, over, over time.

00:10:48.805 --> 00:10:52.015
And then this architecture is 34 layers deep.

00:10:52.015 --> 00:10:56.425
Um, so I thought I'd talk a little bit about the architecture.

00:10:56.425 --> 00:10:59.390
Have you seen ResNets before?

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:04.695
Okay. Uh, so should I go into this?

00:11:04.695 --> 00:11:07.785
I think you can go- they are going to learn about this next, next week.

00:11:07.785 --> 00:11:11.685
Okay. Cool. Here's my one-minute spiel of ResNet then,

00:11:11.685 --> 00:11:14.980
is that you're going deeper in terms of

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:18.595
the number of layers that you're having in a network.

00:11:18.595 --> 00:11:22.660
You should be able to represent a larger set of functions.

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:26.770
But when we look at the training error for these very deep networks,

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:31.105
what we find is that it's worse than a smaller network.

00:11:31.105 --> 00:11:32.725
Now this is not the validation error,

00:11:32.725 --> 00:11:34.255
this is the training error.

00:11:34.255 --> 00:11:39.010
That means even with the ability to represent a more complex function,

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:43.120
we aren't able to represent the training data.

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:48.820
So the motivating idea of residual networks is to say, "Hey,

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:52.060
let's add shortcuts within the network so as to

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:56.960
minimize the distance from the error signal to each of my layers."

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:02.695
Uh, this is just mapped to say the same thing.

00:12:02.695 --> 00:12:06.970
So further work on ResNet showed that, okay,

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:08.650
we have the shortcut connection,

00:12:08.650 --> 00:12:12.955
how should we make information flow through it the best?

00:12:12.955 --> 00:12:17.620
And, uh, the finding was basically that anything you,

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:19.735
you add to the shortcut, to the highway,

00:12:19.735 --> 00:12:22.735
think of these as stop signs or,

00:12:22.735 --> 00:12:26.665
um, or, or signals on a highway.

00:12:26.665 --> 00:12:28.570
And it's basically saying the fastest way on the

00:12:28.570 --> 00:12:31.030
highway is to not have anything but addition on it.

00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:39.385
[NOISE] And then there were a few advancements on top of that, um,

00:12:39.385 --> 00:12:42.220
like adding dropout and

00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:46.104
increasing the number of filters in the convolutional neural network,

00:12:46.104 --> 00:12:49.600
um, that we also add it to this network.

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:53.335
Okay. So that's the convolutional neural network.

00:12:53.335 --> 00:12:55.735
Let's talk a little bit about data.

00:12:55.735 --> 00:13:03.040
So one thing that was cool about this project was that we got to partner up with a- uh,

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:08.215
with a start-up that manufactures these hardware patches and we got

00:13:08.215 --> 00:13:13.405
data off of patients who were wearing these patches for up to two weeks.

00:13:13.405 --> 00:13:18.460
And this was from around 30,000 patients.

00:13:18.460 --> 00:13:22.750
Um, and this is 600 times bigger than the largest data set that,

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:25.265
that was out there before.

00:13:25.265 --> 00:13:28.380
And for each of these ECG signals,

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:35.220
what happens is that each of them is annotated by a clinical ECG expert who says,

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:37.950
"Here's where rhythm A starts and here is where it ends,

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:40.025
so let's mark the whole ECG that way."

00:13:40.025 --> 00:13:43.815
Obviously, very time intensive but a good data source.

00:13:43.815 --> 00:13:46.140
And then we had a test set as well.

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:48.165
And here we used, um,

00:13:48.165 --> 00:13:51.330
here we used a committee of cardiologists.

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:54.550
So they'd get together sit in a room and decide, okay,

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:57.640
we disagree on the specific point, let's try to,

00:13:57.640 --> 00:14:01.870
let's try to discuss which one of us is right or what this rhythm actually is.

00:14:01.870 --> 00:14:05.230
So they arrive at a ground truth after discussion.

00:14:05.230 --> 00:14:08.320
And then we can, of course, test cardiologists as well.

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:11.350
And the way we do this, is we have them do it individually.

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:13.705
So there's not the same set that did the ground truth,

00:14:13.705 --> 00:14:16.555
there's a different set of cardiologists coming in one at a time,

00:14:16.555 --> 00:14:19.090
you tell me what's going on here and we're gonna test you.

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:26.875
[NOISE] So when we compared the performance of our algorithm to cardiologists,

00:14:26.875 --> 00:14:30.955
uh, we found that we were able to surpass them,

00:14:30.955 --> 00:14:32.620
um, on the F1 metrics.

00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:35.150
This is precision and recall.

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:39.250
And when we looked at where the mistakes were made,

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:43.120
uh, we can see that sort of the,

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:47.680
the biggest mistake was between distinguishing two rhythms, which look very,

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:54.175
very similar, um, but actually don't have a difference in, um, in treatment.

00:14:54.175 --> 00:14:59.755
Here's another case where the model is not making a mistake which the experts are making.

00:14:59.755 --> 00:15:01.855
Um, and turns out this is a costing mistake.

00:15:01.855 --> 00:15:04.735
This is saying a benign heart rhythm, uh,

00:15:04.735 --> 00:15:07.570
or what experts thought was a benign heart rhythm,

00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:10.400
was actually a pretty serious one.

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:14.710
Um, so that's, that's one beauty of automation,

00:15:14.710 --> 00:15:17.665
is that we're able to catch these,

00:15:17.665 --> 00:15:19.780
um, catch these misdiagnosis.

00:15:19.780 --> 00:15:24.550
[NOISE] Um, here are three hard blocks, uh,

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:29.575
which are clinically relevant to catch on which the model outperforms the experts,

00:15:29.575 --> 00:15:32.380
and on atrial fibrillation which is probably

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:36.200
