00:18:54.765 --> 00:19:00.945
So here we looked at how we can detect pneumonia off of chest X-rays.

00:19:00.945 --> 00:19:03.870
Uh, so pneumonia is an infection that affects,

00:19:03.870 --> 00:19:07.500
uh, millions in the, uh, US.

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:11.415
Uh, its big global burden is actually in,

00:19:11.415 --> 00:19:13.200
um, in kids.

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:16.820
Uh, so that's where it's really useful to be able to,

00:19:16.820 --> 00:19:19.745
uh, detect that automatically and well.

00:19:19.745 --> 00:19:22.220
So to detect pneumonia,

00:19:22.220 --> 00:19:24.095
there's a chest X-ray exam.

00:19:24.095 --> 00:19:29.105
Uh, and chest X-rays are the most common,

00:19:29.105 --> 00:19:31.335
uh, imaging procedure, uh,

00:19:31.335 --> 00:19:34.545
with two billion chest X-rays done per year.

00:19:34.545 --> 00:19:39.180
And the way abnormalities are detected in chest X-rays is they

00:19:39.180 --> 00:19:43.380
present as areas of increased density.

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:45.690
Uh, so where things should appear dark,

00:19:45.690 --> 00:19:48.750
they appear brighter or vice versa.

00:19:48.750 --> 00:19:54.015
And here's what characteristically pneumonia looks like,

00:19:54.015 --> 00:19:56.475
where it's like a fluffy cloud.

00:19:56.475 --> 00:19:59.355
Uh, but this is an oversimplification, of course,

00:19:59.355 --> 00:20:03.000
because, uh, pneumonia is when the alveoli fill up with pus,

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:06.390
but the alveoli can fill up with a lot of other things as well,

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:09.150
which lead to very different interpretations

00:20:09.150 --> 00:20:12.570
and diagnoses for the patients and treatment for the patient.

00:20:12.570 --> 00:20:14.460
So it's quite confusing which is

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:18.135
why radiologists trained for years to be able to do this.

00:20:18.135 --> 00:20:25.155
Um, the setup is we'll take an input image of someone's chest X-ray and

00:20:25.155 --> 00:20:32.580
output the binary label 01 which indicates the presence or the absence of pneumonia.

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:36.300
And here we use a 2D convolutional neural network,

00:20:36.300 --> 00:20:39.520
uh, which is pre- pre-trained on ImageNet.

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:45.525
Okay. So we looked at shortcut connections earlier and, uh,

00:20:45.525 --> 00:20:48.800
DenseNets had this, um,

00:20:48.800 --> 00:20:52.460
idea to take shortcut connections to the extreme.

00:20:52.460 --> 00:20:58.020
It says what happens if we connect every layer to every other layer instead

00:20:58.020 --> 00:21:00.450
of just connecting sort of one-

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:04.480
instead of having just one shortcut which is what ResNet had.

00:21:04.730 --> 00:21:10.365
And, uh, DenseNet beat the previous state of the art, um,

00:21:10.365 --> 00:21:17.055
and has generally lower error and fewer parameters on the ImageNet challenge.

00:21:17.055 --> 00:21:19.065
So that's what we used.

00:21:19.065 --> 00:21:21.090
Uh, for the dataset,

00:21:21.090 --> 00:21:23.535
when we started working on this project, uh,

00:21:23.535 --> 00:21:28.425
which was around October of last year, uh,

00:21:28.425 --> 00:21:35.325
there was this large dataset that was released by the NIH of 100,000 chest X-rays.

00:21:35.325 --> 00:21:40.635
And this was the largest public dataset at the time.

00:21:40.635 --> 00:21:46.170
And here each X-ray is annotated with up to 14 different pathologies.

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:51.360
And the way this annotation works is there is an NLP system which reads a report,

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:54.750
and then outputs for each of several pathologies,

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:56.340
whether there is a mention,

00:21:56.340 --> 00:21:59.970
whether there is a negation like not pneumonia,

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:04.020
for instance, um, and then annotates accordingly.

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:06.120
And then for our test set, uh,

00:22:06.120 --> 00:22:08.970
we had four radiologists here at Stanford,

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:14.865
independently annotate and tell us what they thought was going on in those X-rays.

00:22:14.865 --> 00:22:19.484
So one of the questions that comes up often in medical imaging,

00:22:19.484 --> 00:22:25.515
is we have, we have a model,

00:22:25.515 --> 00:22:27.165
we have several experts,

00:22:27.165 --> 00:22:29.355
but we don't really have a ground truth.

00:22:29.355 --> 00:22:32.160
And we don't have a ground truth for several reasons sometimes.

00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:37.410
One of them is just that it's difficult to tell whether someone had pneumonia or

00:22:37.410 --> 00:22:43.185
not without additional information like their clinical record,

00:22:43.185 --> 00:22:48.510
or even once you gave them antibio- antibiotics, did they get treated?

00:22:48.510 --> 00:22:54.110
Uh, so really one way to evaluate whether a, uh,

00:22:54.110 --> 00:22:58.715
model is better than a radiologists or as well- doing,

00:22:58.715 --> 00:23:01.235
as well as the radiologist, is by saying,

00:23:01.235 --> 00:23:05.380
do they agree with other experts similarly?

00:23:05.380 --> 00:23:08.385
So that's what we use here, that's the idea.

00:23:08.385 --> 00:23:14.010
We say, okay, let's have one of the radiologists be the,

00:23:14.010 --> 00:23:17.610
the, the prediction model we're evaluating.

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:21.825
And let's set another radiologist to be ground truth.

00:23:21.825 --> 00:23:25.334
And now we're gonna compute the F1-score once,

00:23:25.334 --> 00:23:27.240
uh, change the ground truth,

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:28.635
do it the second time,

00:23:28.635 --> 00:23:30.420
change it again, third,

00:23:30.420 --> 00:23:34.410
and then also use the model as the ground truth and do it again.

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:37.530
And we can use a very symmetric evaluation scheme.

00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:43.935
But this time having the model be evaluated against each of the four experts.

00:23:43.935 --> 00:23:47.265
So we do that and then we get a score for both of them.

00:23:47.265 --> 00:23:50.220
Well, for all of the experts and for the model.

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:53.655
And we showed in our work that we were able to,

00:23:53.655 --> 00:23:58.540
uh, do better than the average radiologist at this task.

00:23:59.390 --> 00:24:03.420
Um, two ways to extend this in the future is to

00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:06.570
be able to look at patient history as well,

00:24:06.570 --> 00:24:08.235
and look at, uh,

00:24:08.235 --> 00:24:13.425
lateral radiographs and be able to improve upon this diagnosis.

00:24:13.425 --> 00:24:17.670
Um, at the time at which we released our work, um,

00:24:17.670 --> 00:24:20.370
on all 14 pathologies,

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:24.760
we were able to outperform the previous state of the art.

