uh, to be able to,

00:31:37.900 --> 00:31:40.615
um, and, and is looking at the right thing.

00:31:40.615 --> 00:31:44.260
So I guess you can say the algorithms- heart's in the right place.

00:31:44.260 --> 00:31:46.360
[LAUGHTER] Cool.

00:31:46.360 --> 00:31:52.795
Uh, but I thought- so this is an image that I got from the,

00:31:52.795 --> 00:31:55.465
the dataset that we were using NIH.

00:31:55.465 --> 00:32:00.715
But it's pretty cool if an algorithm is able to generalize to populations beyond.

00:32:00.715 --> 00:32:04.450
And so I thought what we'd do is we could just look up,

00:32:04.450 --> 00:32:08.920
um, [NOISE] look up an image of cardiomegaly, uh,

00:32:08.920 --> 00:32:15.100
and download it and just see if our model's able

00:32:15.100 --> 00:32:22.700
to- this one looks pretty large, so is this.

00:32:24.540 --> 00:32:27.355
I don't want an annotated one.

00:32:27.355 --> 00:32:29.065
All right. That's good.

00:32:29.065 --> 00:32:38.740
So we can do that, save it, desktop.

00:32:38.740 --> 00:32:42.650
And now we can upload it here.

00:32:47.610 --> 00:32:54.250
And it's already re-done its thing and on the top is cardiomegaly once again.

00:32:54.250 --> 00:32:59.485
[NOISE] So it's able to generalize to- and there's the highlight.

00:32:59.485 --> 00:33:04.855
So it's able to generalize to populations beyond just the ones it was trained on.

00:33:04.855 --> 00:33:08.050
So I'm very excited by that.

00:33:08.050 --> 00:33:12.910
And what I got even more excited by is, uh,

00:33:12.910 --> 00:33:15.950
we're thinking of deploying this out in,

00:33:15.950 --> 00:33:18.135
um, out in different parts of the world,

00:33:18.135 --> 00:33:20.715
and when we got an image, uh,

00:33:20.715 --> 00:33:25.365
that showed how X-rays are read in, uh,

00:33:25.365 --> 00:33:28.380
this hospital that we're working with in Africa,

00:33:28.380 --> 00:33:31.125
uh, this is what we saw.

00:33:31.125 --> 00:33:35.835
And so the idea that one could snap a picture and upload it,

00:33:35.835 --> 00:33:40.180
seems- and get a diagnosis seems very powerful.

00:33:40.640 --> 00:33:45.170
Um, so the third case study I wanna take you through is,

00:33:45.170 --> 00:33:46.720
um, being able to look at MR.

00:33:46.720 --> 00:33:49.180
So we've talked about 1D,

00:33:49.180 --> 00:33:51.640
a 1D setup where we had an ECG signal.

00:33:51.640 --> 00:33:54.610
We've talked about a 2D set-up with an X-ray.

00:33:54.610 --> 00:34:00.595
How many of you thinking of working on a 3D problem for your project?

00:34:00.595 --> 00:34:03.115
Okay. A few. Well, that's good.

00:34:03.115 --> 00:34:08.785
Cool. Um, so here we looked at knee MR.

00:34:08.785 --> 00:34:15.340
So MR's of the knee is the standard of care to evaluate knee disorders,

00:34:15.340 --> 00:34:21.085
and more MR examinations are performed on the knee than any other part of the body.

00:34:21.085 --> 00:34:27.130
Um, and the question that we sought out to answer was,

00:34:27.130 --> 00:34:30.805
can we identify knee abnormalities?

00:34:30.805 --> 00:34:34.480
Um, two of the most common ones include an ACL tear and

00:34:34.480 --> 00:34:39.380
a meniscal tear at the level of radiologists.

00:34:39.660 --> 00:34:42.235
Now with the 3D problem,

00:34:42.235 --> 00:34:45.340
one thing that we have that we don't have in a 2D setting

00:34:45.340 --> 00:34:48.670
is the ability the he loo- to look at the same,

00:34:48.670 --> 00:34:50.905
same thing from different angles.

00:34:50.905 --> 00:34:53.679
And so when radiologists do this diagnosis,

00:34:53.679 --> 00:34:55.015
they look at three views;

00:34:55.015 --> 00:34:58.390
the sagittal, the coronal, and the axial,

00:34:58.390 --> 00:35:01.795
which are, [NOISE] which are three ways of,

00:35:01.795 --> 00:35:04.360
uh, looking through, uh,

00:35:04.360 --> 00:35:06.370
the 3D structure of the knee.

00:35:06.370 --> 00:35:10.990
And in an MR you get different types of series, ah,

00:35:10.990 --> 00:35:13.015
based on the magnetic fields,

00:35:13.015 --> 00:35:15.130
and so here are three different,

00:35:15.130 --> 00:35:18.475
um, series that are, that are used.

00:35:18.475 --> 00:35:26.395
And what we're gonna do is output for a particular knee MR examination,

00:35:26.395 --> 00:35:29.200
the probability that it's abnormal,

00:35:29.200 --> 00:35:31.450
the probability of an ACL tear,

00:35:31.450 --> 00:35:34.030
and the probability of a meniscal tear.

00:35:34.030 --> 00:35:38.065
The important thing to recognize here is this not a multiclass problem,

00:35:38.065 --> 00:35:43.300
and that I could have both types of tears and it's a multi-label problem.

00:35:43.300 --> 00:35:48.370
[NOISE] So we're gonna train a, uh,

00:35:48.370 --> 00:35:54.670
convolutional neural network for every view-pathology pair.

00:35:54.670 --> 00:35:58.494
So that's nine convolutional networks,

00:35:58.494 --> 00:36:00.310
and then combine them together,

00:36:00.310 --> 00:36:04.330
uh, using a logistic regression.

00:36:04.330 --> 00:36:09.205
So here's what each convolutional neural network looks like.

00:36:09.205 --> 00:36:12.385
I have a bunch of slices within a view.

00:36:12.385 --> 00:36:15.580
I'm gonna pass each of them to a feature extractor,

00:36:15.580 --> 00:36:18.530
I'm gonna get an output probability.

00:36:18.540 --> 00:36:25.330
So we had 1,400 knee MR exams from,

00:36:25.330 --> 00:36:29.335
uh, the Stanford Medical Center, and, uh,

00:36:29.335 --> 00:36:32.515
we tested on 120 of them,

00:36:32.515 --> 00:36:34.960
where the majority vote of three,

00:36:34.960 --> 00:36:41.125
uh, subspecialty radiologists established the, the ground truth.

00:36:41.125 --> 00:36:46.270
And we found that we did pretty well on,

00:36:46.270 --> 00:36:48.535
on the three tasks,

00:36:48.535 --> 00:36:54.280
and had the model be able to pick up the different abnormalities pretty well.

00:36:54.280 --> 00:36:55.675
And one can extend these,

00:36:55.675 --> 00:36:58.480
these methods of interpretive- interpretability,

00:36:58.480 --> 00:37:02.425
uh, to, uh, to 3D,

00:37:02.425 --> 00:37:03.760
3D inputs as well.

00:37:03.760 --> 00:37:05.380
So that's what we did here.

00:37:05.380 --> 00:37:09.595
Okay. So, uh, I, I saw this,

00:37:09.595 --> 00:37:11.770
I saw this cartoon a few,

00:37:11.770 --> 00:37:13.870
a few weeks ago and I thought it was,

00:37:13.870 --> 00:37:15.370
it was pretty funny, uh,

00:37:15.370 --> 00:37:18.730
which is a lot of machine learning engineers think,

00:37:18.730 --> 00:37:21.460
uh, that they don't need to externally validate,

00:37:21.460 --> 00:37:25.870
which is to find out how my model works on, uh,

00:37:25.870 --> 00:37:30.340
works on data that's not my- where my original data set came from,

00:37:30.340 --> 00:37:34.060
so there's, uh, there's a difference in, in distributions.

00:37:34.060 --> 00:37:36.430
Uh, but it's really quite, uh,

00:37:36.430 --> 00:37:40.195
exciting when a model does generalize to,

00:37:40.195 --> 00:37:43.400
to datasets that it's not seen before.

00:37:44.250 --> 00:37:47.575
And so we got this dataset that's,

00:37:47.575 --> 00:37:51.235
that's public from a hospital in Croatia.

00:37:51.235 --> 00:37:52.450
And here's how it was different.

00:37:52.450 --> 00:37:53.679
So it was a different,

00:37:53.679 --> 00:37:56.080
it was a different kind of series,

00:37:56.080 --> 00:37:58.150
with different magnetic properties.

00:37:58.150 --> 00:38:03.025
Uh, it's a different scanner and it was a different institution in a different country.

00:38:03.025 --> 00:38:04.675
And we asked, "Okay,

00:38:04.675 --> 00:38:07.870
what happens when we run this model off-the-shelf that was trained on

00:38:07.870 --> 00:38:11.395
Stanford data but tested on that kind of data?"

00:38:11.395 --> 00:38:16.450
And we found that it did relatively well without any training at all.

00:38:16.450 --> 00:38:20.350
[NOISE] But then when we trained on it,

00:38:20.350 --> 00:38:23.800
we found that we were able to outperform the

00:38:23.800 --> 00:38:28.195
previous lead best-reported result on the dataset.

00:38:28.195 --> 00:38:34.035
So there's still some work to be done in being able to generalize, um,

00:38:34.035 --> 00:38:37.350
sort of my network here that was trained on my data

00:38:37.350 --> 00:38:41.595
to be able to work on datasets from different institutions,

00:38:41.595 --> 00:38:43.005
different countries as well,

00:38:43.005 --> 00:38:45.390
but we're making some steps along that way,

00:38:45.390 --> 00:38:47.820
it remains a very open problem for taking.

00:38:47.820 --> 00:38:49.110
[NOISE]

00:38:49.110 --> 00:38:54.535
And it, and it is a very [inaudible] in hospital that has some sort of the [inaudible]

00:38:54.535 --> 00:38:58.030
Yeah. So we did the best we could in terms of processing.

00:38:58.030 --> 00:39:04.075
So we had- so one of the preprocessing steps that's important is being able to, uh,

00:39:04.075 --> 00:39:06.325
get the mean of the,

00:39:06.325 --> 00:39:12.685
of the input data to be as close to the mean of the input data that you trained on.

00:39:12.685 --> 00:39:16.570
Uh, so that was one preprocessing step we tried,

00:39:16.570 --> 00:39:20.725
but we were trying to minimize that to say out of the box, how would this work?

00:39:20.725 --> 00:39:22.600
If we had never seen this data before,

00:39:22.600 --> 00:39:25.430
how would it work on that population?

00:39:26.670 --> 00:39:32.605
So one big topic in across,

00:39:32.605 --> 00:39:36.565
uh, a lot of applied fields is asking the question, okay,

00:39:36.565 --> 00:39:41.920
we're talking about models working automatically autonomously,

00:39:41.920 --> 00:39:51.100
how would these models work in- when working together with experts in different fields?

00:39:51.100 --> 00:39:56.530
And here we asked that questions about radiologists and about imaging models.

00:39:56.530 --> 00:39:59.575
Would it be possible to be able to boost

00:39:59.575 --> 00:40:05.000
the performance if the model and the radiologists work together?

00:40:05.970 --> 00:40:08.440
And so that's really the set-up.

00:40:08.440 --> 00:40:11.080
A radiologist with model,

00:40:11.080 --> 00:40:14.600
is that better than the radiologists by themselves?

00:40:15.090 --> 00:40:17.275
And here's how we set it up.

00:40:17.275 --> 00:40:26.395
We set- let's have experts read the same case twice separated by a certain set of weeks,

00:40:26.395 --> 00:40:32.930
um, and then see how they would perform on the same set of cases.

00:40:33.360 --> 00:40:39.610
And what we found, that we were able to increase the performance generally with

00:40:39.610 --> 00:40:46.315
a signifant- significant increase in specificity for ACL tears.

00:40:46.315 --> 00:40:52.270
That means if someone- if a patient came in, uh, without a,

00:40:52.270 --> 00:40:55.390
uh, without an ACL tear,

00:40:55.390 --> 00:40:59.330
I'd be able to, uh, find it better.

00:40:59.580 --> 00:41:02.785
So in the future- yes, question?

00:41:02.785 --> 00:41:04.270
Would you have any bias,

00:41:04.270 --> 00:41:06.220
the opinion of the radiologist,

00:41:06.220 --> 00:41:08.890
or is that the intended thing that you wanna kind of

00:41:08.890 --> 00:41:12.415
bias in the opinion for that it actually looks at the patient's health?

00:41:12.415 --> 00:41:14.515
Yeah. So that's a good question, and I,

00:41:14.515 --> 00:41:17.740
and I think how- so, uh,

00:41:17.740 --> 00:41:21.670
sort of automation bias captures a lot of this,

00:41:21.670 --> 00:41:28.735
and that once we have sort of models working with, um, experts together,

00:41:28.735 --> 00:41:34.075
can we expect that the experts will sort of take it less seriously because that's,

00:41:34.075 --> 00:41:35.230
that's a big concern,

00:41:35.230 --> 00:41:37.930
and start relying on what the model says and says,

00:41:37.930 --> 00:41:40.255
"I won't even look at this exam.

00:41:40.255 --> 00:41:42.805
I'm just gonna trust what the model says blindly."

00:41:42.805 --> 00:41:47.425
Um, that's absolutely possible in a very open area of research.

00:41:47.425 --> 00:41:51.490
Some of the ways that people have tried to address it is to say,

00:41:51.490 --> 00:41:53.515
"You know what I'm gonna do from time to time?

00:41:53.515 --> 00:41:57.475
I'm gonna pass in an exam to the radiologist for which

00:41:57.475 --> 00:42:01.930
I'm gonna to flip the answer and I'll know the right one.

00:42:01.930 --> 00:42:04.360
And if they get that wrong, I'll alert them,

00:42:04.360 --> 00:42:08.230
that you're relying too much on the model, uh, stop."

00:42:08.230 --> 00:42:13.315
Uh, but there are a lot of more sophisticated ways to go about addressing automated bias.

00:42:13.315 --> 00:42:16.630
And as far as I know, it's a very open field of research,

00:42:16.630 --> 00:42:20.600
especially as we're getting into deep learning assistance.

00:42:21.510 --> 00:42:25.675
And one utility of this is to say

00:42:25.675 --> 00:42:29.020
basically that the set of patients don't need a follow-up,

00:42:29.020 --> 00:42:32.545
let's not send them for unnecessary surgery.

00:42:32.545 --> 00:42:34.450
Great. So I shared,

00:42:34.450 --> 00:42:36.940
uh, three case studies from the lab.

00:42:36.940 --> 00:42:41.500
The final thing I wanna do is to talk a little bit about how you can

00:42:41.500 --> 00:42:45.880
get involved if you're interested in applications of AI to healthcare.

00:42:45.880 --> 00:42:52.000
Uh, so the first is, uh,

00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:55.705
the ability for you to just get your hands dirty with,

00:42:55.705 --> 00:42:59.785
uh, datasets and, and be able to try out your own model.

00:42:59.785 --> 00:43:01.825
So we have, uh,

00:43:01.825 --> 00:43:03.295
from our lab, released,

00:43:03.295 --> 00:43:04.705
uh, the MURA dataset,

00:43:04.705 --> 00:43:06.955
which is a large dataset of,

00:43:06.955 --> 00:43:09.700
uh, uh, bone X-rays,

00:43:09.700 --> 00:43:12.760
and the task is to be able to tell if it's, um,

00:43:12.760 --> 00:43:14.890
if the X-rays are normal or not,

00:43:14.890 --> 00:43:16.914
and they come from different,

00:43:16.914 --> 00:43:20.320
um, parts of the- of the upper body,

00:43:20.320 --> 00:43:25.300
um, and that's- that's what the dataset X-rays look like.

00:43:25.300 --> 00:43:30.430
And this is a pretty interesting setup because you have more than one view,

00:43:30.430 --> 00:43:33.430
uh, so more than one angle for the same body part,

00:43:33.430 --> 00:43:34.480
for the same study,

00:43:34.480 --> 00:43:35.620
for the same patient,

00:43:35.620 --> 00:43:38.620
and the goal is to be able to combine these well, uh,

00:43:38.620 --> 00:43:40.975
into convolutional neural network and,

00:43:40.975 --> 00:43:44.095
and be able to output the probability of an abnormality.

00:43:44.095 --> 00:43:48.475
And one of the interesting things here for transfer learning as well is,

00:43:48.475 --> 00:43:53.460
do you wanna train the models differently per body part or do you wanna train them,

00:43:53.460 --> 00:43:58.355
uh, train the same model for body parts or combine certain models?

00:43:58.355 --> 00:44:01.300
Uh, so a lot of design decisions there.

00:44:01.300 --> 00:44:04.240
And this is what trai- some trained models look like.

00:44:04.240 --> 00:44:08.380
This is a model baseline that we released that's able to

00:44:08.380 --> 00:44:13.610
identify a fracture here and a piece of hardware on the right.

